1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,600
Picture a small clinic on a Monday morning.

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:09,372
The phone is ringing, three patients are waiting at the desk, and the receptionist

3
00:00:09,372 --> 00:00:13,095
books two people into the same doctor’s ten-thirty slot.

4
00:00:13,415 --> 00:00:18,540
Nobody notices until both of them are sitting in the waiting room.

5
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:23,460
That small, very real problem is where this project started.

6
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:28,885
I’m Arjun, and this is ClinicFlow, my DevOps final capstone.

7
00:00:29,205 --> 00:00:32,280
The app is a clinic front-desk system.

8
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:36,200
But honestly, the app is only half the story.

9
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:43,047
The other half is the journey: how a change I make on my laptop ends up running on a

10
00:00:43,047 --> 00:00:49,342
Kubernetes cluster in AWS, tested, scanned, autoscaling and monitored, without me

11
00:00:49,342 --> 00:00:51,595
logging into a single server.

12
00:00:51,995 --> 00:00:54,445
Let me show you the app first.

13
00:00:54,765 --> 00:01:00,319
This is what the receptionist sees: today’s appointments, who has checked in, and

14
00:01:00,319 --> 00:01:01,965
how busy each doctor is.

15
00:01:02,285 --> 00:01:04,935
Booking is a simple form.

16
00:01:05,255 --> 00:01:08,455
Pick a patient, pick a doctor, pick a time.

17
00:01:08,855 --> 00:01:11,530
Now here’s the part I care about.

18
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:17,859
If I try to book the same doctor into a slot that’s already taken, the backend

19
00:01:17,859 --> 00:01:18,475
refuses.

20
00:01:18,795 --> 00:01:25,574
It returns a 409 conflict, and the receptionist sees exactly which appointment is in

21
00:01:25,574 --> 00:01:26,220
the way.

22
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:32,065
The same rule protects patients, so nobody can be in two rooms at once.

23
00:01:32,385 --> 00:01:38,310
And because front desks use whatever device is nearby, it works on a phone too.

24
00:01:38,710 --> 00:01:40,635
So that’s the what.

25
00:01:40,955 --> 00:01:42,630
Now the how.

26
00:01:42,950 --> 00:01:48,663
This is the route every change takes: thirteen stages, from the app itself all the

27
00:01:48,663 --> 00:01:51,450
way to troubleshooting it in production.

28
00:01:51,770 --> 00:01:57,159
I’ll walk through them in order, and I’ll stick to what actually happened, including

29
00:01:57,159 --> 00:01:58,570
the things that broke.

30
00:01:58,970 --> 00:02:01,770
Stage one is the application.

31
00:02:02,090 --> 00:02:09,007
A FastAPI backend with full create, read, update and delete for doctors, patients

32
00:02:09,007 --> 00:02:11,740
and appointments, on PostgreSQL.

33
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:18,059
The schema is managed with Alembic migrations, so the database structure lives in

34
00:02:18,059 --> 00:02:19,910
Git like everything else.

35
00:02:20,230 --> 00:02:24,255
There are two health endpoints, and they do different jobs.

36
00:02:24,575 --> 00:02:28,375
Health only answers, “is the process alive?”

37
00:02:28,695 --> 00:02:31,420
Ready also checks the database.

38
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:37,615
That difference matters later, because Kubernetes only sends traffic to pods that

39
00:02:37,615 --> 00:02:38,340
are ready.

40
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:40,790
Stage two is Git.

41
00:02:41,110 --> 00:02:46,464
Every change is a commit with a message that says what it does, and there are more

42
00:02:46,464 --> 00:02:49,010
than twenty of them in this repository.

43
00:02:49,330 --> 00:02:54,651
The gitignore keeps secrets, environment files and caches out of the history,

44
00:02:54,651 --> 00:02:59,005
because once a secret is in Git, you should treat it as public.

45
00:02:59,405 --> 00:03:03,330
Stage three is the pipeline, in GitHub Actions.

46
00:03:03,650 --> 00:03:09,550
The moment I push to main, it runs the linter and thirty-six automated tests.

47
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:12,095
This is the first gate.

48
00:03:12,415 --> 00:03:17,665
If a single test fails, nothing gets built and nothing gets deployed.

49
00:03:17,985 --> 00:03:20,835
A broken commit simply stops here.

50
00:03:21,235 --> 00:03:23,460
Stage four is Docker.

51
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:27,980
The frontend and the backend are each packaged as an image.

52
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:34,682
The frontend uses a multi-stage build: Node compiles the React app, and only the

53
00:03:34,682 --> 00:03:38,750
finished files are copied into a small Nginx image.

54
00:03:39,070 --> 00:03:42,795
Both containers run as a non-root user.

55
00:03:43,115 --> 00:03:49,022
And for local work, one command, docker compose up, brings up the frontend, the

56
00:03:49,022 --> 00:03:51,265
backend and Postgres together.

57
00:03:51,665 --> 00:03:56,790
Stage five is security, and this is my favourite part of the story.

58
00:03:57,110 --> 00:04:02,919
The pipeline scans every image with Trivy and fails the build on any serious

59
00:04:02,919 --> 00:04:05,135
vulnerability that has a fix.

60
00:04:05,455 --> 00:04:08,230
The first time I ran it, it failed.

61
00:04:08,550 --> 00:04:14,825
Four high-severity CVEs, in urllib3, msgpack and setuptools.

62
00:04:15,145 --> 00:04:19,270
The strange thing was, my app doesn’t use any of them.

63
00:04:19,590 --> 00:04:25,715
They came bundled inside pip, which is only needed while the image is being built.

64
00:04:26,035 --> 00:04:31,555
So instead of patching them, I removed pip from the final image, and all four

65
00:04:31,555 --> 00:04:33,060
findings disappeared.

66
00:04:33,380 --> 00:04:36,955
Sometimes the best fix is deleting something.

67
00:04:37,355 --> 00:04:40,005
Stage six is the registry.

68
00:04:40,325 --> 00:04:45,905
Once the scan passes, the exact image that was scanned is pushed to GitHub’s

69
00:04:45,905 --> 00:04:47,300
container registry.

70
00:04:47,620 --> 00:04:51,820
It’s tagged with the Git commit hash, never “latest”.

71
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:58,310
That means any running container traces back to the exact code that produced it, and

72
00:04:58,310 --> 00:05:01,615
rolling back is just deploying an older hash.

73
00:05:02,015 --> 00:05:04,490
Stage seven is Terraform.

74
00:05:04,810 --> 00:05:11,494
Instead of clicking around the AWS console, the infrastructure is code: a VPC with

75
00:05:11,494 --> 00:05:17,934
public and private subnets across two availability zones, a NAT gateway, an EKS

76
00:05:17,934 --> 00:05:24,782
cluster with two worker nodes in the Mumbai region, and an IAM role that lets GitHub

77
00:05:24,782 --> 00:05:27,635
deploy without any stored AWS keys.

78
00:05:27,955 --> 00:05:33,986
My AWS account is on the free plan, which blocked the usual instance type, so I

79
00:05:33,986 --> 00:05:37,955
switched the nodes to one that’s free-tier eligible.

80
00:05:38,275 --> 00:05:44,000
And when I was done, terraform destroy removed all sixty-five resources.

81
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:50,320
A real cluster costs money every hour, so tearing it down is part of the job.

82
00:05:50,720 --> 00:05:53,145
Stage eight is Kubernetes.

83
00:05:53,465 --> 00:05:59,734
Here’s the app running on EKS: two backend pods, two frontend pods, and Postgres

84
00:05:59,734 --> 00:06:03,965
with its own storage volume, spread across both nodes.

85
00:06:04,285 --> 00:06:10,027
If a pod dies, its deployment replaces it, and services give the pods a stable

86
00:06:10,027 --> 00:06:13,560
address, so it doesn’t matter which one answers.

87
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:16,085
Stage nine is Helm.

88
00:06:16,405 --> 00:06:22,593
Rather than hand-editing a pile of YAML files, the whole deployment is one chart:

89
00:06:22,593 --> 00:06:28,705
deployments, services, ingress, the autoscaler, a network policy and monitoring.

90
00:06:29,025 --> 00:06:34,894
The pipeline runs one helm upgrade with the new commit’s tag, and if the new pods

91
00:06:34,894 --> 00:06:38,300
never become healthy, it rolls back on its own.

92
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:41,200
Stage ten is the ingress.

93
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:48,450
One entry point, two routes: the root path goes to the React frontend, and slash-api

94
00:06:48,450 --> 00:06:49,770
goes to FastAPI.

95
00:06:50,090 --> 00:06:56,623
I used Traefik as the ingress controller, because the old ingress-nginx project was

96
00:06:56,623 --> 00:06:58,040
retired this year.

97
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:05,115
One honest caveat: the AWS free plan also blocks public load balancers, so for this

98
00:07:05,115 --> 00:07:09,185
demo I reached the ingress through a port-forward.

99
00:07:09,505 --> 00:07:12,405
The routing is exactly the same.

100
00:07:12,805 --> 00:07:19,155
Stage eleven is autoscaling, and here I wanted proof, not just configuration.

101
00:07:19,475 --> 00:07:23,375
So I ran a load generator inside the cluster.

102
00:07:23,695 --> 00:07:30,208
CPU on the backend climbed to more than three times what each pod had requested, and

103
00:07:30,208 --> 00:07:36,567
the horizontal pod autoscaler responded: two pods, then three, four, five, six, in

104
00:07:36,567 --> 00:07:38,195
about ninety seconds.

105
00:07:38,595 --> 00:07:41,120
Stage twelve is monitoring.

106
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:47,381
The backend exposes Prometheus metrics, including a couple I added myself, like how

107
00:07:47,381 --> 00:07:49,815
many double-bookings were blocked.

108
00:07:50,135 --> 00:07:55,835
Prometheus scraped all six pods during that test, and every target was up.

109
00:07:56,155 --> 00:08:02,068
Grafana showed it live: around three hundred and ninety requests a second, zero

110
00:08:02,068 --> 00:08:08,055
errors, and a ninety-fifth percentile latency of about ninety-five milliseconds.

111
00:08:08,455 --> 00:08:12,830
Stage thirteen is troubleshooting, because things will break.

112
00:08:13,150 --> 00:08:16,450
I wrote three deliberately broken setups.

113
00:08:16,770 --> 00:08:21,495
A wrong image tag, which shows up as an image pull back-off.

114
00:08:21,815 --> 00:08:28,425
A service whose label selector has a typo, so it has no endpoints and quietly drops

115
00:08:28,425 --> 00:08:29,540
every request.

116
00:08:29,860 --> 00:08:35,185
And a wrong database password, which leaves a pod stuck in a crash loop.

117
00:08:35,505 --> 00:08:41,437
Each one is a small lesson in reading the events, the endpoints and the logs before

118
00:08:41,437 --> 00:08:42,080
guessing.

119
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:47,655
Looking back, the most useful moments weren’t the ones that worked first time.

120
00:08:47,975 --> 00:08:53,764
Pinning the Trivy action to an exact commit, after its version tags were hijacked

121
00:08:53,764 --> 00:08:55,050
earlier this year.

122
00:08:55,370 --> 00:08:59,520
Working around the limits of the AWS free plan.

123
00:08:59,840 --> 00:09:06,940
Figuring out why GitHub’s new identity tokens didn’t match my AWS trust policy.

124
00:09:07,260 --> 00:09:10,835
That’s the part that felt like real engineering.

125
00:09:11,235 --> 00:09:13,410
So that’s ClinicFlow.

126
00:09:13,730 --> 00:09:19,962
One commit sets off tests, a security scan, an image build, a registry push and a

127
00:09:19,962 --> 00:09:25,655
Helm deploy to a cluster that scales itself and reports on its own health.

128
00:09:25,975 --> 00:09:29,475
Every running pod traces back to a commit.

129
00:09:29,795 --> 00:09:31,720
Thanks for watching.

